1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,670
Since we would like our algorithm to generalize to unseen dataset during training,

2
00:00:05,670 --> 00:00:11,295
we're going to focus on the performance of our algorithm on the test dataset.

3
00:00:11,295 --> 00:00:14,070
In particular, we are interested in

4
00:00:14,070 --> 00:00:19,235
the test error which is the error rate of our model on the test dataset.

5
00:00:19,235 --> 00:00:23,545
The test error can be decomposed into three terms.

6
00:00:23,545 --> 00:00:26,099
The variance, the bias,

7
00:00:26,099 --> 00:00:29,420
and epsilon which we will ignore for the moment.

8
00:00:29,420 --> 00:00:32,720
The variance describes the amount by which

9
00:00:32,720 --> 00:00:37,400
our model's parameters would change if we were to change the training data.

10
00:00:37,400 --> 00:00:42,700
The bias describes how good our model is given the training data.

11
00:00:42,700 --> 00:00:44,825
To minimize the test error,

12
00:00:44,825 --> 00:00:49,940
we need to minimize both of bias and the variance which

13
00:00:49,940 --> 00:00:56,100
means that we need our model to both perform well on the training data and the test data.

14
00:00:56,100 --> 00:00:59,150
However, by minimizing the bias,

15
00:00:59,150 --> 00:01:04,175
we tend to increase the variance because we are over-fitting the training data set.

16
00:01:04,175 --> 00:01:06,620
Let's look at an example together.

17
00:01:06,620 --> 00:01:08,450
We are fitting a model on

18
00:01:08,450 --> 00:01:12,590
a particular dataset and we can control the complexity of this model.

19
00:01:12,590 --> 00:01:15,655
For example, by increasing the number of parameters.

20
00:01:15,655 --> 00:01:18,620
Well, as the model becomes more complex,

21
00:01:18,620 --> 00:01:22,340
the bias in red decreases.

22
00:01:22,340 --> 00:01:26,240
Our model has more capacity to fit the training data.

23
00:01:26,240 --> 00:01:33,130
However, the variance in purple increases with the complexity as

24
00:01:33,130 --> 00:01:35,900
our model becomes more and more specialized to

25
00:01:35,900 --> 00:01:40,090
this dataset and loses its capacity to generalize.

26
00:01:40,090 --> 00:01:42,170
If we sum both terms,

27
00:01:42,170 --> 00:01:45,480
we obtain the test error in blue.

28
00:01:48,290 --> 00:01:52,100
The blue curves show the existence of

29
00:01:52,100 --> 00:01:57,820
an optimal complexity where the test error is minimized.

30
00:01:57,820 --> 00:02:02,210
Well, this is what we call the bias-variance trade-off.

31
00:02:02,210 --> 00:02:05,090
You want your model to be complex enough to have

32
00:02:05,090 --> 00:02:11,130
a low bias but not too complex so that you can still generalize to unseen data.

